.TH "NOTIFICATION.ACTIONS" "3JS" "May 25, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Notification.actions \- Notification: actions property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]actions\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]Notification\f[R] interface provides the actions available for
users to select when interacting with the notification.
.SH VALUE
A read\-only array of actions.
Each element in the array is an object with the following members:
.TP
\f[B]action\f[R]
A string identifying a user action to be displayed on the notification.
.TP
\f[B]title\f[R]
A string containing action text to be shown to the user.
.TP
\f[B]icon\f[R]
A string containing the URL of an icon to display with the action.
.TP
\f[B]navigate\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
A string containing a URL to navigate to when the user activates this
action.
When set, the user agent navigates to this URL instead of firing the
\f[CR]notificationclick\f[R] event.
See \f[CR]Notification.navigate\f[R] for more information.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Notification actions are buttons or controls that appear within
persistent notifications.
They are set using the \f[CR]actions\f[R] option of the second argument
of the \f[CR]showNotification()\f[R] method.
Note that actions are not available for non\-persistent notifications.
If you pass an \f[CR]options\f[R] object with an \f[CR]actions\f[R]
property that is anything other than \f[CR]null\f[R] to the
\f[CR]Notification()\f[R] constructor, a \f[CR]TypeError\f[R] is thrown.
.PP
Clicking the button associated with an action navigates to the URL set
in the \f[CR]navigate\f[R] option if one is specified.
Otherwise, it fires a \f[CR]notificationclick\f[R] event on the service
worker that includes the action that was selected (and the associated
\f[CR]Notification\f[R] instance), so the worker can handle it without
the user ever switching to your page.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] Browsers typically limit the maximum number of actions
they will display for a particular notification.
Check the static \f[CR]Notification.maxActions\f[R] property to
determine the limit.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code shows how a service worker might listen for the
\f[CR]notificationclick\f[R] event and use it to retrieve both the
clicked action and an array of all actions.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// sw.js\f[R]
self.addEventListener(\(dqnotificationclick\(dq, (event) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  \f[B]const\f[R] clickedAction = event.action; \f[I]// e.g. \(dqreply\(dq or \(dq\(dq if body was clicked\f[R]

  \f[I]// Read all defined actions\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] notification = event.notification; \f[I]// the Notification object\f[R]
  console.log(notification.actions); \f[I]// full array of action objects\f[R]

  notification.close();
});
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
Using the Notifications API
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]Notification.maxActions\f[R]
